// What the exe says about itself to the Windows shell: its icon and its
// name. This is the Windows counterpart of packaging/linux's .desktop and
// packaging/macos' Info.plist -- neither of those is linked into anything,
// but on Windows the metadata lives inside the binary.
//
// platitude.res is the compiled form and is what build.rs hands the
// linker. After changing anything here (or platitude.ico), rebuild it with
// the Windows SDK resource compiler, from this directory:
//
//   rc.exe /nologo /fo platitude.res platitude.rc
//
// This file stays ASCII, comments included: rc.exe reads it in the system
// codepage unless told otherwise, so a non-ASCII byte here would compile
// differently on someone else's machine than it does on the one that last
// rebuilt the .res.

// The file's icon: what Explorer shows for it, what a pinned shortcut
// carries, and what the taskbar falls back to before the window is up. The
// running window sets its own separately (src/winframe.rs).
//
// Resource id 1 is deliberate: Windows shows the numerically lowest ICON
// resource as the file's icon, so this has to stay the lowest one here.
1 ICON "platitude.ico"

// The display name. Without a version resource the shell has no name to
// show and falls back to the file name, so the taskbar's window menu read
// "platitude-gg.exe". FileDescription is the field it reads: measured with
// AssocQueryString/ASSOCSTR_FRIENDLYAPPNAME, which answered the bare file
// name for the resourceless exe and notepad.exe's FileDescription for
// notepad.exe, and again through the shell property system (Explorer's
// file-description column). Both now read "Platitude GG".
//
// Do not check that with PowerShell's Get-Item .VersionInfo: .NET's
// FileVersionInfo reports every string field empty for this resource while
// reading its fixed block fine, and a probe with a non-zero FILEVERSION
// changed nothing. Win32 VerQueryValue returns all four strings from the
// same bytes, so the resource is sound and that reader is not.
//
// InternalName and OriginalFilename carry the slug instead, the way Exec=
// and CFBundleExecutable do: those two are machine-read, and the display
// name does not belong in them (design rules, the app-name section).
//
// No version fields. The workspace version lives in Cargo.toml and nothing
// restates it -- Info.plist leaves a @VERSION@ placeholder for the
// packaging step for that reason, and this file cannot do even that,
// because the compiled .res is checked in and linked as it stands.
// Stamping a real version belongs to the packaging step (P5 notes). Saying
// FILEVERSION 0,0,0,0 here would be a no-op anyway: it is what rc.exe
// writes when the statement is absent, byte for byte.
1 VERSIONINFO
FILEOS 0x4      // VOS__WINDOWS32
FILETYPE 0x1    // VFT_APP
BEGIN
    BLOCK "StringFileInfo"
    BEGIN
        // US English, Unicode -- the app ships English only (CLAUDE.md).
        BLOCK "040904B0"
        BEGIN
            VALUE "FileDescription", "Platitude GG"
            VALUE "ProductName", "Platitude GG"
            VALUE "InternalName", "platitude-gg"
            VALUE "OriginalFilename", "platitude-gg.exe"
        END
    END
    BLOCK "VarFileInfo"
    BEGIN
        VALUE "Translation", 0x409, 1200
    END
END
